{"judgments": [
  {"clause_id": "c001", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c002", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This grant defines only \"Said Land,\" yet the operative provisions throughout (Paragraphs 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 15, 19 and 20) impose rights and duties by reference to the undefined term \"Leased Premises.\" Whether the two terms are coextensive, and whether \"Leased Premises\" shrinks with the partial terminations worked by Paragraph 4, is left open.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c003", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The habendum turns on production \"in commercial quantities,\" a phrase the lease nowhere defines despite using it as the trigger in Paragraphs 3(g), 4(a), 4(b), 4(d) and 6. Its meaning (profit over operating costs? over all costs?) is the classic point of construction in a secondary-term dispute.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.38}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c004", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The oil royalty is stated twice over and inconsistently within one sentence: \"the value of the gross production\" is then equated to \"the proceeds received by Lessee.\" A market-value measure and a proceeds measure can diverge, and the sentence does not say which governs when they do.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.44}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c005", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Where Lessee itself purchases the royalty oil there is no third-party sale, so the price term \"at the same proceeds received by Lessee for the sale thereof\" is circular. It is unclear whether Lessee must pay a market or arm's-length price or may set the price by its own purchase.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.31}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c006", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The transportation carve-out is grammatically broken (\"Any deduction for the expenses ... shall be added to the price received\" with an unclosed parenthetical reading \"limited to direct for amounts of Lessor's gas transported\"), so the scope of the permitted regulated-pipeline deduction cannot be read off the words. Whether \"gathering\" and \"compression\" charged by an affiliate midstream company fall inside or outside the no-deduction rule is the central ambiguity.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.58},
    {"issue": "The royalty base is \"proceeds received from any sale of such gas at the point of sale or delivery,\" which sits against Paragraph 3(f)'s command that royalties be delivered free of gathering, compression, treating and marketing costs \"except as provided for in 3(b).\" The two must be reconciled to know whether the valuation point can be moved downstream by netting costs out of the sale price.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.36}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c007", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The floor is set by \"proceeds received from sales in the field or prevailing area whichever is the greater,\" and neither \"field\" nor \"prevailing area\" is defined or bounded. Nor is it said whose sales, over what period, are to be compared.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.42}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c008", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The trigger — gas \"sufficiently impregnated\" that \"paying quantities\" can be liquefied \"as a practical lease operation\" — states no measurable threshold and leaves the duty to install separators to argument. Whether Lessee ever owed the installation obligation is therefore indeterminate on the face of the clause.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.24}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c009", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The clause forbids \"any deductions for any indirect or direct costs\" but then supplies a parenthetical list (\"amortization and depreciation on pipeline and plant investment and indirect overhead associated therewith\"), inviting the argument that only the enumerated costs are barred. The general ban and the specific enumeration cannot both be given full effect.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.40}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c010", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The floor requires computing \"the royalties that would have been paid ... under Paragraph 3(b) if the gas were marketed pursuant to such Paragraph 3(b),\" a counterfactual the clause gives no method for calculating. Whether the comparison uses actual residue-gas prices, field prices, or the 3(b) minimum-proceeds test is unstated.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c011", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Free use ... for operations hereunder\" does not say whether it reaches only operations on Said Land or extends to operations on pooled lands or off-lease facilities such as compressors and plants. The boundary of the free-use privilege is the ambiguity.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.25}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c012", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Computing royalty after deducting production used is in tension with Paragraph 3(f), which requires royalties to be delivered free of production, compression, treating and processing costs. Whether fuel gas burned in compression is a permitted volumetric deduction or a forbidden cost is the question.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.22}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c013", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The blanket no-cost rule is subject to an exception \"except as provided for in 3 (b) above,\" but Paragraph 3(b) is itself a no-deduction provision containing only a narrow regulated-transportation carve-out. What that cross-reference excepts — the regulated transportation charges only, or all costs netted from 3(b) proceeds — is the point a court would have to construe.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.50}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c014", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Shut-in royalty is available for wells \"capable of producing gas in commercial quantities,\" an undefined standard that does not say whether capability is measured with or without pipeline connection and marketing facilities. The lease's continuance under Paragraph 4(d) turns on that same untested phrase.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.30}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c015", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The renewal deadline — \"yearly on or before the expiration of the last day of the year following the preceding year for which such payment or tender has been made\" — is circular and does not identify the anniversary date. It cannot be squared word-for-word with the preceding sentence's \"at yearly intervals\" after a ninety-day first payment.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.32}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c016", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This sentence caps post-primary-term shut-in maintenance at \"two consecutive years,\" but the next sentences (3(g) increased $750 payment and the three-year cumulative cap) presuppose shut-in maintenance continuing past two years. The two-year limit and the three-year limit cannot both be given effect.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.44}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c017", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Drilling unit\" is fixed to Section 15-72-302 of the Arkansas Code, yet Paragraph 4(g) permits a retained drilling unit to include additional acreage when a larger spacing pattern is prescribed. Which meaning controls when a field-wide order enlarges units is unresolved.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.26}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c018", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The escalation to $750 \"[f]ollowing the expiration of the two (2) year shut-in period\" assumes the lease survives that period, while the immediately preceding sentence says shut-in payment maintains the lease \"for a period not to exceed two consecutive years.\" The increased-payment sentence has no field of operation unless that cap is read away.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.42}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c019", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This three-year cumulative cap contradicts the earlier sentence of 3(g) limiting post-primary-term shut-in maintenance to two consecutive years. A court would have to decide whether the outer limit is two years or three.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.38}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c020", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Exempting shut-in payments from proportionate reduction cannot easily be squared with Paragraph 5's rule that on pooled production Lessor receives only its acreage share, or with Paragraph 16's proportionate-reduction covenant. Whether a full $500 per well is owed where Lessor owns a fractional or pooled interest is open.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c021", "issues": [
